  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 1243 PM CDT SAT SEP 12 2026\n\nCurrent cloud activity somewhat resembles something post-frontal\ngiven the movement, and clear slot along the Mississippi Delta\nregion. While no true cold front has moved through the region,\nthe weak trough overhead has deepened slightly more, allowing for\nthis SW to NE movement. Given our region's proximity to this\ntrough, additional showers and thunderstorms are expected today,\ngenerally moving west to east. Given unseasonably high\nprecipitable water (PWAT) values, confidence is high that\nrainfall rates this afternoon will be moderate to heavy.\nThankfully, severe weather and flash flooding concerns remain\nlimited, with most of this rain activity remaining nothing more\nthan beneficial.\n\nUpper-level ridging will quickly build in over the region by\nSunday, returning rain chances to the summertime, diurnally\ndriven pattern we've been stuck in for these many weeks. Due to\nthe ridge, rain chances will remain low for most of the next week\n(<10%), and afternoon highs will quickly begin to rebound into\nthe low to mid-90s\n\n/44/",
